# Line endings, stated once, so git stops guessing per machine.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows wants CRLF in the working tree
# while every blob in this repo is already stored with LF -- so git announced
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on essentially every `git add`, and the working
# tree drifted into a mix (README.md CRLF, everything else LF) depending on
# which tool last wrote a file. The warning was harmless; the mix is the part
# worth removing, because it decides whether a diff shows one changed line or
# the whole file.
#
# eol=lf rather than native: every tool here is node, the site is served by
# Cloudflare, and the recorders and scripts all write LF already. One ending
# everywhere means a file edited on Windows and a file written by a script are
# the same file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Media and fonts are never text. Git usually detects this, but `* text=auto`
# above is an invitation to try, and a single mangled byte in an mp4 is a
# how-to video that no longer plays. The recorder rewrites all 68 of them at
# once, so this is the line that keeps that from ever being a normalisation
# question.
*.mp4  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary
*.docx binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
